- op: discuss  # 8a6a
  at: '2026-09-18T09:58:52Z'  # 8a6a
  by: agent  # 8a6a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8a6a
  clock: 3  # 8a6a
  nonce: 8a6a  # 8a6a
  message: "SATISFIED in PR #1044 (dev cc153c42b6), under the owner's 2026-09-18 ruling: the\nthree present-tense parity claims are RETRACTED and the two arms are documented\nas carrying independent identities; parity is NOT engineered.\n\nWHAT IS STILL TRUE, BY DECISION: parity holds for 0 of 52 shared functions on\naardvark-dns, exactly as this row's title says. The title's condition was not\nremoved; it was re-documented as the shipped state. The owner chose fix shape\n(b) from this row's own description. Shape (a) — match on start_line — was\nNOT taken because split_within_file_stable_id_collisions would re-mint the\nagreeing record as a second SITE, and sparing cross-backend groups encodes\nthe coexist/merge/replace answer that is WI-gojum's (parked). Engineering\nparity is therefore WI-gojum's, not this row's residual.\n\nTHE INVARIANT NOW HOLDS. Its antecedent — \"documented as assigning the same\nstable_id\" — is false everywhere it was true: README §\"Stable-ID parity\"\nrewritten; CHANGELOG 7.0.0 entry corrected IN PLACE (not rewritten); spec\nstable_id v6->v7 history corrected; docstrings in rust_scip.py, translate.py\nand analyzer.py (three sites) state the abstention and point at WI-gojum.\nIts consequent — \"the test must consume producer-shaped input\" — holds:\n  * test_rust_scip_stable_id.py: the baseline-fed test is re-titled\n    test_helper_reproduces_rust_py_ids_when_given_the_item_span (contract 1,\n    the helper's extraction), and\n    test_recorded_rust_analyzer_spans_reach_parity_only_for_single_line_items\n    feeds ranges RECORDED from `rust-analyzer scip` 1.94.0 (4a4ef49) on\n    RUST_SAMPLE itself: five multi-line callables abstain, the single-line\n    trait declaration (25-25) reaches parity.\n  * test_translate.py: the hedged test asserts the overwrite outright; a new\n    production-shaped case pins the retained SCIP id for a 3-line function\n    fed the token span.\nBEHAVIORAL EVIDENCE that these are controls: mutating the predicate to\n`if node_start != start_line:` fails exactly those two tests (2 failed, 24\npassed); restoring it passes all 26. They go red the day the fix lands and\nare re-pointed then.\n\nNOT CLAIMED: that parity is the right design (unchanged from filing).\n\nADDENDUM, found after the PR was cut and recorded here so the next reader\ndoes not re-derive it: rust-analyzer 1.94.0 POPULATES `Occurrence.enclosing_range`\non every Definition occurrence (20 of 20 on the recorded sample), and it IS the\nitem range — `increment` range=[13,11,20] (token) vs enclosing_range=[13,4,16,5]\n(lines 14-17). scip/index.py builds Symbol.span from `range`, never reads\n`enclosing_range`. So the token-span cause of this row has a one-line cure\n(span the Symbol from enclosing_range when present), after which the parity\nhelper WOULD match — and split_within_file_stable_id_collisions would then\nre-mint the SCIP record as a second SITE. The obstacle to parity is therefore\nONLY the WI-gojum ruling now, not a missing input. Filed the Symbol.span\ndefect separately (see the row referencing this one) with an isbefore on\nWI-gojum."  # 8a6a
